[Music]  september 10th  freedom day in denmark  that day the nordic country ditches all  covet curves  fifty thousand danes celebrate the first  post pandemic free concert in copenhagen  [Applause]  i only thought about covet 19 for a  second when i arrived like wow there's a  lot of people here but i've totally  forgotten about it it's just so awesome  to be here  denmark is the first country in europe  to drop all coronavirus restrictions  vaccine passports are no longer needed  to enter bars restaurants or nightclubs  74 of all people are fully vaccinated  and with a very low covet reproduction  rate officials say the virus is now  under control  welcome to your covert 19 special i'm  chris kober in berlin  no longer a socially critical disease  that is the danish government's  assessment of covet 19. after more than  540 days with restrictions to limit the  spread of the coronavirus denmark's high  vaccination rate has enabled citizens to  return to pre-pandemic life at least  inside the  country this is where the man works  whose job it is to explain the pandemic  to the danes virologist alan rundrup  he's convinced that the rapid lockdown  in march 2020 spared the country the  worst  i actually i was in the  studio one of the television channels  the evening that our prime minister  announced that we would have the  complete shutdown of denmark i think it  was the right thing and actually a big  part of the reason  why we have  have had such a relatively mild cause  compared to others  now the government has announced that  covet 19 no longer poses a national  threat and suddenly the corona passes  and masks have vanished from denmark's  streets  the main reason for this radical opening  is the high vaccination rate among  citizens and the authorities detailed  information campaign and all measures  taken  among the most vulnerable of our  patients and citizens the vaccination  rate is more than 9 out of 10. and the  general population is more than 8 out of  10. one thing that we  i think we did very good here and that  people might learn from is the  transparency of the effort but it might  also be the culture that that when the  danish healthcare authorities recommend  something usually the danish people do  it german's vaccination rate is quite a  bit lower as a citizens trust in the  authorities  would make a complete opening such as in  denmark less advisable  now throngs of joyful danes are headed  back to amusement parks  many of them are pleased with the  government's handling of the crisis i  believe that they've handled it from  the intention of doing it best possible  so i think  on the whole i think they've done well  [Applause]  covert testing continues  some experts propose monitoring waste  water to localize future outbreaks while  germans might balk at this kind of  surveillance the danish state is already  highly digitalized making it better  equipped to respond to the pandemic  we have a register of everybody who  tests positive in a laboratory with the  disease we also have a register of who's  vaccinated and we have regis a  population register so we know where  everybody lives and we can contact  people directly when i talk to  colleagues in other countries i can  really see that that has given us an  edge that is a particular thing of  denmark which have helped us  but these days many ordinary danes are  diving headlong back into the lifestyles  they were living before  and hoping they finally got uncovered  under control  let's talk about how the danes did it  with camilla holten-muller she is a  medical doctor and head of the expert  group for mathematical modeling of  covid19 at denmark's stanton's serum  institute welcome to dw camilla for the  moment the pandemic seems to be under  control what are the key elements that  denmark apparently got right in order to  get to this point  i think very importantly in the  springtime we had a really large test  capacity we did a massive test screening  both in schools and in  workplaces  which allowed us simply to to track the  the infected persons very  locally and we had some local lockdowns  that also worked very effectively which  we were able to do simply because of  this  screening test or test strategy we had  in denmark  there seems to be a predominant feeling  of trust  among the danish public trust when it  comes to authorities  and the way they've been handling the  pandemic where the distrust stem from  i think historically uh the danes in  general have a very high trust in in the  health authorities and also in in the  other vaccination campaigns for instance  vaccination of children there's a very  high trust and support of the program  and i think that really also helps us in  the current situation with covet  in many places people are skeptical when  it comes to getting vaccinated against  the coronavirus now looking at denmark's  high vaccination rate which is one of  the highest in the world things appear  to be different  somewhat  why is that  i think first of all is definitely  related to the high trust both in health  authorities but also in general to to to  vaccination programs and vaccinations in  general but i think we had some really  nice campaigns here in denmark and  really informing very nicely about the  the vaccines and and a lot of  transparency and also that this is a  free choice to get vaccinated i think  that has helped us a lot  and camilla particularly young people  now among them the vaccination rate is  lower than the total average  if they can now go back to clubs and  bars without any restrictions could they  become the new drivers of this pandemic  well i take it that no doubt about it  that the unvaccinated will definitely be  a driver of the the epidemic  and especially also children who are not  to be vaccinated children below 12  um amongst the young ones especially is  their way of behaving of course going to  clubs and bars where they're really  close together but definitely that will  be an issue and of course we try to  strive towards having the highest  vaccination coverage as possible in  those groups as well  does denmark specifically target these  people who are eligible for a  vaccination but have chosen not to get  the job so far  yes there is definitely a large campaign  going on so first of all making a  vaccination available  we have vaccinations in high schools we  have vaccinations in supermarkets so  vaccination is  really available everywhere you have  drop-in vaccination but of course also  campaigns on social media which is also  important to to reach out to these  groups  now more than  around three quarters of the danish  population are fully vaccinated what  does that mean  going into the fall and going into the  winter where infections rose in the past  i think what is really important in that  story is as well is that we have a very  high vaccination coverage especially in  the risk groups and in old people who  are at risk of having a severe covet  situation a covet infection that is  really important and of course it's also  important that we just in general have a  high coverage in and above 50 years old  as well  um  that that is the main  goal  camilla having regained their freedom  would the danes go back into lockdown if  infections spiked again  of course i think they would if it  became necessary but i don't think it  will become necessary i mean we have a  really good experience that we can do  well with local lockdowns and also we  have proven that this really a large  test system allows us to to do more  uh local lockdowns instead of these  large national lockdowns i think we will  go  further with that and that will  hopefully be successful in case it  should be needed  carmilla holden miller of the staten  serum institute in copenhagen coming up  thank you for talking to us  now while the danes may be further ahead  than others on their way out of the  pandemic there is the issue of how much  longer we are going to have to deal with  this here is our science correspondent  derek williams answering one of your  questions  how long do most pandemics last  to make any predictions about how the  covet 19 pandemic could end  it makes sense  to look at past pandemics and since they  provide data that could potentially help  us predict  how long the current one might last  experts trying to do that  tend to focus on the four officially  recognized respiratory disease pandemics  that have occurred in the last hundred  years which were all driven by an old  enemy of humanity um influenza  the most recent one was in 2009 when a  novel flu strain commonly called the  swine flu  swept the globe infecting by some  estimates up to one in five people on  the planet uh top end guesses say it  might have caused close to six hundred  thousand deaths  in the 14 or so months that that it  raged the swine flu pandemic was shorter  than a flu pandemic in in 1968 often  called um the hong kong flu  it was caused by a strain that killed at  least a million people worldwide  possibly many more  all in all it lasted a little less than  two years  a decade before that a strain that  became widely known as the asian flu  killed a similar number of people in in  the late 1950s now what was really  interesting about that pandemic  was that it was the first where vaccines  played a key role it's thought that  their quick development and distribution  saved potentially hundreds of thousands  of lives  finally  there was the granddaddy of all modern  flu pandemics the one that devastated  the planet at the end of the first world  war um starting in around 1918.  it's viewed  as the most severe pandemic in recent  history that flu strain infected we  think around a third of the world's  population  and killed at least 50 million people  over the course of around  three years so  to sum it up  those recent pandemics lasted between  around a year and and three years but  they were of course  caused by flu viruses not coronaviruses  so  so trying to draw parallels with the  current pandemic experts say  that is a tenuous move at best  [Music]  and that's our show for more check out  our website at dw.com  kovit19 for now thanks for watching stay  safe  [Music]

